# The Project
This project tests your ability to understand an existing system and extend it. In this README you will find all the information you need to complete the challenge. **Estimated time: 1 hour of coding.** You are free to use any AI tools (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot, etc.).

---

## Context

> **This is the second part of your interview.** In the design challenge, we explored how orders arrive from Uber into the Instaleap (CORE) system. Now we flip the direction: an order has been picked, and we need to notify Uber of the final item changes before closing the loop.

At Instaleap, our team — Tessy — owns the integration layer between Instaleap's internal systems and external marketplaces. Our job is to translate Instaleap's vocabulary into each marketplace's API contract.

In this system, when a picker finishes collecting an order, CORE emits a `picking_finish` event. **Tessy intercepts it, translates the item changes, and calls Uber's replace-items API** so Uber can update the order on their end.

![alt text](Flow.png)

---

## Running the mock environment

The `instaleap-environment-mock` folder contains a mock that simulates both the Uber API and the CORE event publisher. It requires Docker.

```bash
cd instaleap-environment-mock
./run.sh          # start the mock
./clean.sh        # reset if something looks wrong
```

All testable calls are in the [Postman collection](Interview.postman_collection.json).

**Prerequisites:** Node.js, Docker, your preferred IDE.

---

## What you need to implement

Everything that needs to be done is marked with a `// TODO` comment. We recommend tackling them in order:

### TODO — `uber.service.ts`: Call the Uber API

Implement `replaceItems`. This method receives an order ID and the full replacement payload and must PUT it to Uber's endpoint. The Postman collection shows the expected request shape. The base URL (`uber_base_url`) and auth headers are already wired up.

### TODO (stretch) — `receive_order_picking.use_case.ts`: Map valid replacements

Implement `buildValidReplacementPayload`. A valid replacement means the picker substituted the original item with a similar one (e.g., Dolex → Noravel). Use `itemCategorizer.getValidSubstitutesOfAnItem()` to retrieve the first valid substitute and build the Uber payload.

**Expected payload shape:**

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| `row_number` | original `item.id` |
| `replacement_type` | `MENU_ITEM_REPLACEMENT` |
| `count` | substitute's `found_quantity` |
| `name` | substitute's `name` |
| `price.amount` | substitute's `price × 100` (cents) |
| `price.currency` | `currencyCode` (from constructor) |

---

## Codebase structure

```
src/
  models/
    job.ts              ← CORE event types (WebhookItem, WebhookJobEvent)
    uber_payloads.ts    ← Uber API payload types
    interactor.ts       ← UseCaseResponse
  services/
    uber.service.ts     ← TODO
  use_cases/
    receive_order_picking.use_case.ts  ← TODO
    tools/
      item_categorizer.ts  ← already implemented, available to use
  routes/
    routes.ts           ← wired up, no changes needed
```

Feel free to ask your interviewers any questions at any time — they are here to help.
